@app.cell(hide_code=True)
def _(mo):
    mo.md(r"""
    ## Course description

    U.S. healthcare publishes a massive amount of public data on quality, costs, and patient outcomes. In this seminar, you'll build your own data layer from that real data, prove every number in it can be trusted, and put it to work in an app and an AI agent of your own design. Along the way, you'll learn when to trust AI and where human judgment has to make the call. Optional extras are there for anyone who wants to go further, from multi-gigabyte hospital price files to predictive models. No programming experience is required: you'll use the AI tools of your choice to build, and learn to verify that what you built is right.

    The course has one standard: **you direct the AI, and you defend every number it gives you.** Healthcare analytics fails quietly. A wrong denominator, two reporting periods mixed together, or a suppressed value read as zero can sit under a polished app without anyone noticing. You will learn to catch those failures, not type code.
    """)
    return


@app.cell(hide_code=True)
def _(mo):
    mo.md(r"""
    ## What you will be able to do

    By the end of the course you will be able to:

    1. Build an aggregated healthcare data layer from two or three public sources, directing an AI tool to do the building.
    2. Document where every source came from, what period it covers, how often it refreshes, and whether it is current enough for your question.
    3. Trace any number in your work back to the source row it came from, and explain its numerator, denominator and suppression rules.
    4. Judge whether a data source fits a question and a population, and say plainly what it cannot answer.
    5. Tell public aggregate data from protected health information, and apply that line to what you share with AI tools.
    6. Build and publish an app and an AI agent on your data layer, test where the agent's answers go wrong, and hand the app over to someone else.
    7. Explain which decisions in an analytics project belong to a person, and who signs off.
    """)
